            "text": "{\n  \"study_topic_id\": \"study-bf905ef50ed0f725b67f\",\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"text\": \"LIGO (the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory) is a U.S. National Science Foundation-supported research facility, not a telescope that collects light. Its two widely separated detectors—at Hanford, Washington, and Livingston, Louisiana—each use an L-shaped pair of 4-kilometer vacuum arms. Laser beams travel along the arms and recombine; a passing gravitational wave slightly changes the arm lengths and the resulting light pattern. Comparing signals at both sites helps researchers distinguish cosmic events from local disturbances. In September 2015, LIGO made the first direct detection of gravitational waves, from two merging black holes, confirming a new way to study the universe; the achievement was recognized by the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics.\",\n    \"source_urls\": [\n      \"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/space-time\",\n      \"https://ligo.org/gravitational-wave-science/\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"key_facts\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"LIGO’s name stands for Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/space-time\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Each LIGO detector has two 4-kilometer arms arranged in an L.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/space-time\",\n        \"https://ligo.org/gravitational-wave-science/\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The detectors split and recombine laser light; changes in the light pattern reveal tiny changes caused by passing gravitational waves.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://ligo.org/gravitational-wave-science/\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The two U.S. sites are in Hanford, Washington, and Livingston, Louisiana; comparing their data helps check whether a signal is astrophysical rather than local noise.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/space-time\",\n        \"https://ligo.org/gravitational-wave-science/\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"LIGO’s first direct gravitational-wave detection, in 2015, came from merging black holes; the achievement was recognized with the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/space-time\",\n        \"https://ligo.org/gravitational-wave-science/\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"sources\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Exploring Space-Time With NSF LIGO\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/space-time\",\n      \"publisher\": \"U.S. National Science Foundation\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24T18:50:40.570494+00:00\",\n      \"supports\": \"LIGO’s full name and NSF connection; its two U.S. locations and 4-kilometer L-shaped vacuum arms; first detection from colliding black holes in 2015; and the 2017 Nobel recognition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Gravitational-Wave Science\",\n      \"url\": \"https://ligo.org/gravitational-wave-science/\",\n      \"publisher\": \"LIGO Scientific Collaboration\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24T18:50:40.570494+00:00\",\n      \"supports\": \"How laser interferometry measures passing waves, why signals from multiple detectors help distinguish real events from noise, and the first detection’s timing and black-hole-merger origin.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"research_queries\": [\n    \"site:ligo.caltech.edu LIGO what is LIGO first detection gravitational waves interferometer\",\n    \"site:nobelprize.org 2017 physics prize LIGO gravitational waves\",\n    \"site:nsf.gov LIGO observatories arms 4 kilometers gravitational waves\",\n    \"site:nsf.gov LIGO first detection 2015 laser interferometer observatories\",\n    \"site:nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2017/summary LIGO gravitational waves\",\n    \"LIGO education site interferometer arms gravitational wave official NSF\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The overview is 99 words. Claims are grounded in the directly opened NSF and LIGO Scientific Collaboration pages; no tournament material was used as evidence.\",\n  \"unresolved_concerns\": [],\n  \"self_check_complete\": true\n}"
